  and I'll scale it down.

  First question, and it's the one that determines everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "persists across sessions" means a completely different mechanism depending on which half of this repo gets
  the feature. These are not variations on one design — they share no storage medium, no async model, and no failure
  cases.

  - [1mBrowser (webapp)[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m/[38;5;153mIndexedDB[39m, synchronous, per-device, no server. Cheap and self-contained, but
    preferences never follow the user to another browser or machine, and anything stored is readable by any script on
    the page. Fits the login-form app that actually exists.
  - [1mNode CLI[0m — a JSON file under [38;5;153m~/.config/[39m or similar, read at startup, written on change. Also cheap, survives across
    CLI invocations, but it's per-machine and the CLI currently has nothing a user would configure.
  - [1mServer-side, keyed by user[0m — preferences follow the account anywhere. This is the only option that makes "user
    preferences" mean the logged-in user rather than the browser profile — but there is no server here, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a
    stub returning [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m, so it means building auth and a backend first. Much larger scope.

  Cheap to change later: the specific keys and defaults. Expensive: the surface and whether prefs are device-scoped or
  account-scoped — switching from device to account later means a migration and a real backend.

  [1mMy recommendation: the browser webapp with [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, behind a small module that hides the storage medium.[0m It
  matches the code that's actually here, needs no infrastructure, and if you later add a real backend, the module's
  interface is the seam you swap — callers don't change.
